<Layout title="The Idea — We The People" introLines={['The Idea']}>
  <PageHero lines={['The Idea']}>
    <p class="hero__kicker fade-up">Section 01 of the strategy</p>
  </PageHero>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2 class="prose__opener">We all know things are going wrong. We all feel it.</h2>

    <p class="lead">
      Britain in 2026 feels like she's in a tough place. We The People is a vision of how
      we can change our fortunes and our future together. The task is enormous. But this
      could be the first step.
    </p>

    <p>
      Everyone has their own list: cost of living, inequality, immigration, child poverty,
      global conflict — or maybe it's the potholes.
    </p>
    <p>As a result, many feel powerless, angry, afraid or disillusioned.</p>

    <p class="pull">So where can we find hope?</p>

    <h2>System Failure</h2>
    <p>
      The people supposedly in charge — our politicians — seem to be struggling too. So we
      don't know where to turn.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some keep faith with traditional parties. Others turn to populist outsiders. Many
      don't bother to vote at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      Who can blame them? Whoever we vote for, everything keeps getting worse. No wonder
      trust in politicians is the lowest it's ever been.
    </p>
    <p>Yet there is another way. A possibility we have overlooked.</p>

    <h2>We The People can do it ourselves</h2>
    <p>
      Just think of the collective energy, wisdom and creativity among all 70 million of
      us.
    </p>
    <p>
      Imagine what could happen if we brought that collective genius to bear. We might
      discover that our challenges are not as insurmountable as they seem.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our current system is an obvious obstacle to realising that potential. But there is
      another, equally significant: how divided we are.
    </p>

    <h2>Healing Divisions</h2>
    <p>
      Stoked by divisive media, economic pressure and social alienation, our society feels
      more polarised than ever.<a href="#fn1" id="fn1-ref">*</a> Politicians increasingly
      pitch to those extremes. The centre cannot hold.
    </p>
    <p>
      But We The People still believe in democracy. That we can find a way to talk,
      compromise and forge a shared vision for our future, no matter how difficult that
      might seem right now.
    </p>
    <p>Thankfully a powerful democratic mechanism exists to do just that.</p>

    <h2>It's called a Citizens' Assembly</h2>
    <p>In simple terms it applies the principles of jury service to politics.</p>
    <p>
      A group of 100 or so ordinary people are selected at random to reflect the
      population as a whole by factors like class, age, gender, race etc.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are given a difficult societal problem to face. They listen to experts and
      witnesses from all sides of the debate, and then talk among themselves to forge
      policy approaches that everyone can accept.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it works</h2>
    <p>
      Normal people are at the heart of the process so the public feel better represented
      than by an ego-driven political class.
    </p>
    <p>
      Free from the pressures to win votes or funding, an assembly's decisions have
      greater legitimacy too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most importantly, impartial facilitation allows every perspective to be heard. That
      creates room for understanding, respect — even empathy.
    </p>

    <p class="pull">We start to believe in “us” again.</p>

    <h2>It is already happening</h2>
    <p>
      Across the world, assemblies are already at work, helping to resolve previously
      intractable political problems.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Ireland, one led to the legalisation of abortion. In France to an agreement on
      assisted dying. We've even had one in the UK on climate change that led to rare
      agreement between right and left.
    </p>
    <p>
      This democratic mechanism has deep historical roots — and now offers hope for a
      peaceful, united path through this hour of crisis.
    </p>
    <p>This is how we can turn things around.</p>

    <p class="footnote" id="fn1">
      * <a
        href="https://www.ipsos.com/en-uk/uks-sense-division-reaches-new-high-culture-war-tensions-grow-study-finds"
        rel="noopener">Ipsos: UK's sense of division reaches new high as culture war
        tensions grow</a
      >
    </p>

